## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design per-tenant QoS with dynamic config in a multi-DC setup.

## Key Concepts
- Traefik RateLimit middleware, Redis store, per-tenant quotas, Docker Config
- TLS termination, hot-reload without restart, cross-DC coordination
- Request tagging with X-Tenant and validation of isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo middleware sketch: extract tenant and enforce quota from Redis
async function enforceTenantRate(req, res, next){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  const quota = await redisGetQuota(tenant);
  if (quota.allow(req)) return next();
  res.status(429).send('Too Many Requests');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit cross-DC quota drift and recover from Redis replication lag?
- How would you test hot-reload behavior under config churn?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design end-to-end trust from image provenance to runtime policy enforcement, plus canary control and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Content Trust/Notary for image provenance
- SPIFFE/SVIDs via Vault for mTLS
- OPA policies loaded via Docker Config
- Sigver sidecar for per-request validation
- Canary rollout with latency/error thresholds and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo example: verify image signature before starting app
async function verifySignature(image) {
  const sig = await notary.verify(image);
  if (!sig || !sig.valid) throw new Error('invalid image');
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle signature revocation and key rotation in this setup?
- How would you scale the sigver and policy sidecars across nodes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-DC data residency controls, real-time policy updates, and secure audit logging in a docker-dca setup.

## Key Concepts
- Data residency and cross-DC egress policies
- WASM-based per-tenant policy engine (Wasmtime)
- Vault-driven tenant configs and SPIFFE SVID rotation
- SIEM-based auditing and reproducible bootstrap

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo policy evaluation sketch
function evaluate(event, tenant) {
  if (event.destDC === 'DC-B' && !tenant.optIn) return 'block';
  redact(event.payload, tenant.schema);
  return 'allow';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify policy correctness across DC failover?
- How would you handle policy module hot-reload and versioning?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to drive feature-flag configurations from Docker Configs in a multi-DC setup, plus per-tenant routing and safe fallbacks.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Configs for per-tenant flags
- X-Tenant header routing and fallback strategies
- In-process caching with TTL and config-driven feature gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js Express snippet to load tenant flags from docker config mounted at /config
app.use((req,res,next)=>{
  const t = req.header('X-Tenant');
  let flags = cache.get(t);
  if(!flags){
    try{
      const raw = fs.readFileSync(`/config/feature-flags-${t}.json`, 'utf8');
      flags = JSON.parse(raw);
      cache.set(t, flags);
    }catch(e){
      flags = globalDefaults;
    }
  }
  req.flags = flags;
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you invalidate tenant flag cache when the Docker Config changes without restarting the API?
- How would you test this across two DCs with Traefik and simulate Config updates?
